<p>I'm used to the terms Nationalists and Communists to differentiate between the sides of the Chinese Civil War and their respective parts of the United Front. If it's confusing, I can change it.</p>
<p>The factory is, admittedly, not the focus of the piece. It's meant to provide the dilemma to the Ethics Committee, which is whether to use a totally benign anomaly to aid a civil population, but while directly aiding a belligerent nation.</p>
<p>I think Nanjihg and Leningrad are pretty much unprecedented in the modern era in the scale of civilian lives on the line. The point of including Nanjing is twofold - it shows that the Foundation has done this before, and it gives E-5 something to be spiteful about when it comes down to voting. The article isn't intended to be about the ethical question. it's about the Ethics Committee is full of flawed people (or has a tendency to exacerbate people's flaws), and has no oversight, To the point where, to some, condemning an entire city to starvation is the obvious, logical solution, and that the entire process is tainted with petty politics between members.</p>

