			<div class="content" id="post-content-3678610">
									

<p>Tentative upvote. The core concept (&quot;Ethics committee squabbles over food-maker as Leningrad starves&quot;) is great; the execution is solid &#8212; that being said, I feel like this article ought to have either been significantly longer or significantly shorter. Some thoughts:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Some of the recordings are great, but their usage here felt a bit out of place. Also, although I quite liked the inner squabbling of the EC (the antagonism between E-5 and E-2 over Nanking vs Leningrad felt credible, to me), the lack of discussion over alternative solutions made the proceedings feel flat. I understand that there's probably a good reason they can't distribute the cans anonymously, or amnesticize government officials afterward &#8212; but the fact that no one even brings it up made it feel less credible.</p>
<p>One thing that came up that I liked: Mentioning the political fallout of discovery. I think this in particular is how you sell the opposition as having a credible point. Yes, we can amnesticize officials; yes, we can distribute food anonymously. But what if GRU P notices? Maybe not <em>all</em> records of SCP-3554's existence were destroyed. Maybe they know something about a food-producing anomaly that's been lost, and a sudden surplus of <em>borscht</em> &#8212; with these particular labels, no less! &#8212; would clue them in. I mean, they're not idiots; they're going to notice if Leningrad <em>doesn't</em> starve. And maybe the Foundation can't afford to have GRU P sniffing around.</p>
<p>Then the conversation becomes less 'we have to hide anomalies at all costs' and more 'what's the consequence of having GRU P discover we're hiding anomalies from them right under their own noses?'. That, to me, is a far more interesting discussion &#8212; because it's one where you can actually see the opposition's point of view. Maybe early-40s Foundation isn't equipped to handle an antagonistic anomalous government organization. Maybe they've got a prior agreement with GRU P and this containment is violating it. Maybe they need GRU P's cooperation for another project, or containing other SCPs &#8212; and losing it could be catastrophic for the Foundation as an organization.</p>
<p>Either way, I feel like this would have worked better if we either 1) Never got to see the conversation where they condemned Leningrad to starve, or 2) Saw that conversation expanded and more focused on why letting Leningrad starve might be the Foundation's only politically viable option.</p>
<p>That being said, it's always fun and neat to see skips focused both on history and ethics, so &#8212; again! &#8212; tentative +1.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

